      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 367921416,
      "version": 407,
      "versionNonce": 1319447304,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1791790995744,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "## Array Indexing\n\nThere are no physical indexes stored with each element in an array.\n\nThe index is used to **calculate the memory address** of an element.\n\n### Formula:\n\n**Address of arr[i] = Base Address + (i × Size of Data Type)**\n\nWhere:\n\n* **Base Address** = Address of the first element (`arr[0]`)\n* **i** = Index of the element\n* **Size of Data Type** = Number of bytes occupied by one element\n\n### Example:\n\nSuppose:\n\n```text\nBase Address = 1000\nint size = 4 bytes\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```text\nAddress of arr[0] = 1000 + (0 × 4) = 1000\nAddress of arr[1] = 1000 + (1 × 4) = 1004\nAddress of arr[2] = 1000 + (2 × 4) = 1008\nAddress of arr[3] = 1000 + (3 × 4) = 1012\n```\n\nTherefore, arrays provide **O(1) time access** because the address of any element can be calculated directly using the formula.\n\n### Important Point\n\n**Searching** an element by value is generally **O(n)** in an unsorted array.\n\nBut **accessing** an element using its index is **O(1)**.\n\n**Access:** `arr[i]` → O(1)\n**Search:** Find a value → O(n) (unsorted array)\n\n5. Using these indexes random access is allowed .\n\n6. Array is an object created at run time and lives inside heap area .\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "## Array Indexing\n\nThere are no physical indexes stored with each element in an array.\n\nThe index is used to **calculate the memory address** of an element.\n\n### Formula:\n\n**Address of arr[i] = Base Address + (i × Size of Data Type)**\n\nWhere:\n\n* **Base Address** = Address of the first element (`arr[0]`)\n* **i** = Index of the element\n* **Size of Data Type** = Number of bytes occupied by one element\n\n### Example:\n\nSuppose:\n\n```text\nBase Address = 1000\nint size = 4 bytes\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```text\nAddress of arr[0] = 1000 + (0 × 4) = 1000\nAddress of arr[1] = 1000 + (1 × 4) = 1004\nAddress of arr[2] = 1000 + (2 × 4) = 1008\nAddress of arr[3] = 1000 + (3 × 4) = 1012\n```\n\nTherefore, arrays provide **O(1) time access** because the address of any element can be calculated directly using the formula.\n\n### Important Point\n\n**Searching** an element by value is generally **O(n)** in an unsorted array.\n\nBut **accessing** an element using its index is **O(1)**.\n\n**Access:** `arr[i]` → O(1)\n**Search:** Find a value → O(n) (unsorted array)\n\n5. Using these indexes random access is allowed .\n\n6. Array is an object created at run time and lives inside heap area .\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
